 "declaration": "DEMO_READY_FOR_FAB",
 "findings": [
  {
   "detail": "it read 'actual 2.5 mm X', which is the pre-FBV2-EXP-002 figure from the 70.000 mm board.  Measured on 1a06b058: outline X 0.000..72.000 with the D-709 step to 77.000 between Y 70.500..104.005, against an 80.000 x 155.000 mm cavity placed to the locked 1.500 mm west gap -- east gap 6.500 mm at the 72 mm sections and 1.500 mm AT THE STEP.  D-239 says the gap 'falls 2.5 -> 1.5 mm on both sides, the >= 1.5 mm rule met EXACTLY, with nothing to spare', which is a materially different statement from the one the spec was carrying.",
   "fixed": "DEVICE_SPEC row corrected to >= 1.5 mm, actual MINIMUM 1.500 mm X and 3.500 mm Y, with the measurement and its provenance",
   "what": "DEVICE_SPEC's board-to-cavity clearance row was stale by TWO revisions"
  },
  {
   "detail": "measured against 1a06b058: 104 of its 324 rows no longer match the board, 14 references in it are no longer on the board (D3 R42 R51-R54 R57-R60 R130 TP14 TP41 U23) and 5 board references are not in it (C84 C85 D14 Q11 R132).  BOSS1's row still carried the pre-D-759 40.000.",
   "fixed": "a header marks it a SNAPSHOT with those counts and names the board and the CPL as the coordinate authority.  D-758 is what happens when a superseded coordinate is read as a current one.",
   "what": "FBV2_P1_COORDINATES.csv reads like a current coordinate source and is a milestone snapshot"
  },
  {
   "detail": "FBV2_P1_KEEPOUTS section 4 cites 'the >= 15 mm IR TX<->RX rule' as the reason D1 cannot move, and D-226 widened IR_BARRIER 3.0 -> 5.0 mm to stand between them.  Measured: 15.1327 mm centre to centre, MET by 0.1327 mm; D1's easternmost pad at x 54.290 and U6's westernmost at 64.210, so the 57.500..62.500 barrier stands cleanly between them.",
   "fixed": "mechanical_keepout_contract MK9, with a control that nudges the pair 0.200 mm together -- more than the margin -- and is refused",
   "what": "the IR emitter/receiver separation rule had 0.133 mm of margin and nothing was checking it"
  }
 ],
